<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Can We Help the Environment? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> An Environmental Project</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> How Can We Help the Environment?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Coordinate roles and tasks for a group project.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain rules for recycling (e.g., 'Everyone needs to bring their own bag.'). Can coordinate roles for the environmental project with classmates (e.g., 'You bring markers, and I'll bring paper.'). Can ask and answer logistics questions on recycling task (e.g., 'Do we wash bottles before putting them in recycling?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different environmental actions (e.g., planting trees, recycling bins, cleaning a park). Students, in pairs, discuss what they see and say one thing they do to help the environment. "What do you see?" "How can we help?" (e.g., "I see trees." "We can plant trees.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for coordinating tasks for an environmental project. "I will bring the gloves." "You will bring the water." "Please dig carefully." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of 'will' /wɪl/ and its contracted form 'I'll' /aɪl/, emphasizing the difference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If I say, "You will bring the plants," are you bringing them now or later? (Later.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If I say, "Wash the plastic bottles," is that an instruction or a question? (An instruction.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "An Environmental Project".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue script or audio recording titled "Planning Our School Garden Project." The dialogue features two 6th-grade students, Maria and Carlos, coordinating tasks for their upcoming project. It includes sentences with future simple, imperatives, and logistical questions. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Dialogue Snippets:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria: "So, for our school garden project, what will we do first?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carlos: "First, we will clean the field. Then, we will dig the soil."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria: "Okay! You bring the small shovels. I'll bring the seeds. Do we water the plants every day?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carlos: "Yes, we care for the plants daily. Remember, plant the trees near the fence."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discuss environmental actions in group discussions, such as proposing ways to reduce plastic waste. Give instructions and suggestions. Explain how to recycle.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Speaking-focused):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What project are Maria and Carlos planning?" (A school garden project.) "What are they talking about?" (Their plan and tasks.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role Matching (oral):</b> Teacher lists tasks on the board (e.g., "bring shovels," "bring seeds," "dig soil," "water plants"). Students say "Maria will do this," or "Carlos will do that."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Future simple tense for plans ('We will plant trees tomorrow.'), Imperatives for instructions ('Bring a shovel to the park.'), Present simple to describe everyday events ('We wash the plastic and glass bottles every day.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> project, environment, classroom, plan, field, garden, trees, plants, meeting, trip, event, celebration, competition, presentation day, due date, participate, finish, submit, review, complete, plant, present, grow, water, dig, clean, care, prepared, excited, local, stressed, ready, future, upcoming, improved, bring, plant, dig.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focus on 'will' /wɪl/ and 'I'll' /aɪl/. Segmenting 'presentation' into 'pre-sen-ta-tion'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Give instructions and suggestions ('Let's recycle paper.'), Explain how to recycle ('Put paper in the blue bin.'), Describing everyday events ('We usually sort the bottles by color and size.').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Choral/Individual Repetition):</b> The teacher displays incomplete sentences related to the project. Students complete them orally, focusing on future simple and imperatives.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"Tomorrow, we ___ (plant) trees." (Expected: "Tomorrow, we will plant trees.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"___ (Bring) your own bag for recycling." (Expected: "Bring your own bag for recycling.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">"We always ___ (sort) the plastic bottles." (Expected: "We always sort the plastic bottles.")</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher listens for and corrects pronunciation of 'will' and 'I'll'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Substitution Drill in Pairs):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher provides sentence frames and word options on the board. One student says the frame, the other substitutes words to create a new sentence.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Frame: "I will ___ the ___." Options: (plant, trees), (bring, paper), (clean, classroom).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Frame: "Let's ___ the ___." Options: (dig, garden), (water, plants), (care for, environment).</li>
                        </ul>
                        Pairs practice asking and answering, e.g., "What will you bring?" "I'll bring markers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Instructions & Questions):</b> Students imagine they are preparing for a "Recycling Event." In pairs, they take turns giving instructions and asking logistical questions.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Bring blue bins for paper."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Do we wash bottles before putting them in recycling?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Yes, we wash them first. Put paper in the blue bin."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Students use vocabulary like 'project,' 'clean,' 'sort,' 'wash,' 'bins,' 'bottles,' 'paper.'</li>
                        </ul>
                        Peer check on clear pronunciation of 'will' and 'I'll'.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "An Environmental Project".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group role-play and presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form groups of 3-4. Each group is assigned a mini "Environmental Project" scenario (e.g., "Plan a Classroom Recycling System," "Organize a Small Garden in the School Yard," "Prepare for a Beach Clean-up Trip").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to coordinate roles and tasks for their project. They must decide who will do what, what materials they will bring, and what instructions are necessary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must use future simple ("I will...", "You will..."), imperatives ("Bring...", "Clean...", "Plant..."), and ask/answer logistical questions ("Do we need...?", "What will you do?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 5-7 minutes of group discussion and planning, each group will present their coordinated plan to the class. One student from the group can present or they can take turns explaining their roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages the use of vocabulary: 'project', 'plan', 'garden', 'trees', 'plants', 'clean', 'care', 'bring', 'markers', 'paper', 'gloves', 'ready'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reminds students to focus on clear pronunciation, especially of 'will' and 'I'll'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their group's coordinated plan, explaining roles and tasks to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce target vocabulary and structures (especially 'will'/'I'll') clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 5-7 words from the 'project'/'environment' theme (e.g., project, plan, plant, bring, clean, water).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of coordination:</b> Students clearly explain who will do what using future simple and imperatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Group members actively participate and interact to coordinate roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully present a coherent plan for their assigned project.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts orally or on a small piece of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one task I explained to my group using "I will" or "You will"?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one new word I used today to talk about our environmental project?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy about coordinating roles for our environmental project with my classmates?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult? How can I improve my speaking to give instructions and suggestions next time?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., Students actively used future simple and imperatives in their group discussions; pronunciation practice of 'will' and 'I'll' was effective.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with asking logistics questions; need to provide more sentence starters for questions. Ensure all students participate equally in speaking tasks.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson prepared students to coordinate roles and tasks. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use these coordinated plans to write a short paragraph outlining their group's environmental project, including roles, materials, and steps, applying the same vocabulary and grammar structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>